## Part 2 — The moving parts

### Files

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `main.py` | The entire app |
| `.streamlit/config.toml` | Brand theme colors. Read by Streamlit at startup. Not secrets. |
| `.devcontainer/EPGS-HorizontalLogo-Color.svg` | The logo in the page header |
| `requirements.txt` | Python packages Streamlit Cloud installs |
| `docs/MAINTENANCE.md` | This file |

### Secrets

Set in Streamlit Cloud under **Settings → Secrets**. Never in the repo.

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `API_KEY` | JotForm API key. Without it nothing can be pulled. |
| `ADMIN_PASSWORD` | Unlocks the Settings and Debug tabs. If unset, `?admin=1` on the URL unlocks them instead, which is fine for testing and bad for production. |
| `overrides_sheet_url` | URL or ID of the Google Sheet |
| `[gcp_service_account]` | Google service account credentials block. The sheet must be shared with the `client_email` inside it, with Editor access. |

### `main.py`, section by section

| Section | What lives there |
| --- | --- |
| 1 — The form registry | `FORMS`. Every tracked form, all in one place. **This is the only place you edit to add or remove a form.** |
| 2 — Detail form columns | The extra columns the "who did it" forms add |
| 3 — Location names | Aliasing and fuzzy suggestion. No hardcoded location list. |
| 4 — Quotas and operating days | How a required count is resolved for one site |
| 5 — Google Sheet persistence | Reading and writing the four tabs |
| 6 — Answer parsing | Flattening JotForm's name and date widgets |
| 7 — JotForm API | Fetching with retries, and `extract_row()` |
| 8 — Location health | Finding dropped submissions |
| 9 — Compliance math | Turning submissions into percentages |
| 10 — Branding | Palette, logo, CSS |
| 11 — Session setup | Secrets, admin gate, loading the sheet |
| 12 — Tabs | Report, Location Health, Settings, Debug |

### Functions worth knowing

| Function | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `extract_row(sub, form_id)` | One JotForm submission becomes one flat row. Stores `location_raw` only; does **not** alias. |
| `apply_location_aliases(df, aliases)` | Resolves `location_raw` into `location`. Runs on every render, so alias edits apply to cached pulls. |
| `fetch_rows_cached(...)` | The cached, concurrent pull. 20-minute TTL. |
| `get_all_data(...)` | Pull plus aliasing. Deliberately does **not** filter by location, so mismatches survive to be counted. |
| `_redact(text, api_key)` | Strips the API key out of anything shown on screen. Error messages from `requests` contain the full request URL, key included. |
| `compute_period_targets(...)` | The compliance math. One row per (form, location). |
| `build_location_health(...)` | What the Location Health tab shows. |
| `_validate_registry(FORMS)` | Runs at startup. Catches registry typos before they produce wrong numbers. |

### The form registry

Each entry in `FORMS` is keyed by JotForm form ID:

```python
"223424048202040": {
    "name": "Lot Audit",
    "compliance": {"quota": 3, "interval": "daily"},
    "location_fields": ["location"],
    "location_source": "static list",
    "detail": None,
},
```

- `name` — used everywhere, including in the Google Sheet. Renaming it
  orphans any Overrides or Operating Days rows that use the old name.
- `compliance` — `None` means tracked and reported but never scored.
- `location_fields` — JotForm's internal field names, in priority order.
  First one with an answer wins.
- `location_source` — `"reusable dropdown"` or `"static list"`.
- `detail` — `None`, or a block for forms that record people's names.

#### "Why does `location` appear in eight different forms?"

That is not a duplicate. JotForm field names are scoped to a single form, so
`location` on Lot Audit and `location` on Site Audit are two unrelated
questions that happen to share a name. What would be a bug is the same name
twice **inside one list**, and `_validate_registry()` refuses to start if that
happens.

#### "Why do most forms list two location fields?"

When a form moved to the reusable dropdown it got a **new** question for it.
The old dropdown was hidden rather than deleted, because deleting it would
have wiped the location off every historical submission. So the list is
`[new widget field, old retired field]` and whichever has an answer wins. That
is what lets one report cover both old and new submissions.

